# Vandeta MITM payload - evil-mitm: take one device offline, without deauth.
#
#   MITM Attack > Payload > evil-mitm.mitm > Target > the test phone > Start
#
#   ... or from the console, which is how the tests drive it:
#       mitm payload /BruceSpoof/evil-mitm.mitm
#       mitm target 192.168.1.50
#       mitm run 60 /BruceSpoof/evil-mitm.mitm
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   The ARP poison already puts this board between the target and its gateway.
#   `forward off` then makes sure nothing is bridged back, and the DNS rule
#   answers every lookup with 0.0.0.0 so each name fails instantly instead of
#   waiting for a timeout. To the phone this looks exactly like a network with
#   no internet: "Connected, no internet", pages fail in a second, no captive
#   portal appears.
#
# WHY IT EXISTS
#   A deauth burst is the usual way to knock a device off, and it is what the
#   ucmitm twin relies on. It stops working on modern clients: 802.11w (PMF) is
#   mandatory on WPA3 and enabled by default on current iOS, so those deauth
#   frames are dropped as unprotected management frames - an iPhone 13 stays
#   associated while an old Android drops at once. This payload never touches
#   802.11 management frames. It needs one thing instead: the network password,
#   because the board has to be an associated client for the poison to be
#   encrypted and accepted. With that it behaves the same on WPA2, WPA2/WPA3
#   mixed and WPA3-SAE alike, and the client has no way to tell.
#
# TARGET IS NOT SET HERE ON PURPOSE
#   A payload without a `target` line poisons every host the scan found. That is
#   never what a test wants on a live network, so pick the one device first
#   (menu: Target, or `mitm target <ip>`); `mitm status` shows the mode.
#
# TO RESTORE
#   `mitm stop` (or BACK / stopping the run) re-ARP every pair, so the target is
#   back online in under a second. If a run is killed hard, waiting for the ARP
#   cache to expire restores it too.
#
# WANT A PAGE INSTEAD OF A BLACKOUT? Replace the last two lines with:
#   forward on
#   page * html/no-internet.html
# which keeps the target online but answers every plaintext request with the
# notice page - useful to prove on the phone's screen that the engine is the
# thing in the middle.
forward off
dns * 0.0.0.0
